  {
   "xml_index": 5,
   "xml_name": "PS9_005_Longest Rising Stretch",
   "display_name": "Longest Rising Stretch",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>A weather station writes down the temperature at noon every day. A <b>rising stretch</b> is a group of <b>consecutive</b> days where each day is <b>strictly warmer</b> than the day before. One single day on its own counts as a rising stretch of length 1.</p>\n<p>For example, for these temperatures the longest rising stretch is the 5 days <code>20, 21, 24, 26, 30</code>, starting at index <code>1</code>:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">temps =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">25</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(37,105,163);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">20</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(43,114,173);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">21</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(49,124,183);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">24</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(56,134,193);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">26</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(62,144,203);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">30</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">28</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">29</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">5</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">6</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">7</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">the blue days: 20 &lt; 21 &lt; 24 &lt; 26 &lt; 30, starting at index 1, length 5</div>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>longest_rise(temps)</code> that takes a <b>non-empty</b> list of integers and <b>returns</b> a tuple <code>(start, length)</code>: the index where the longest rising stretch starts, and how many days it has. If several stretches are equally long, return the one that starts <b>first</b>. Note that equal temperatures (like <code>20, 20</code>) do <b>not</b> count as rising.</p>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> go through the days from index 1, keeping track of where the <b>current</b> rising stretch started. If today is warmer than yesterday, the current stretch goes on; otherwise a new stretch starts today. Each time, check if the current stretch is longer than the best one so far.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul><li><code>longest_rise([25, 20, 21, 24, 26, 30, 28, 29])</code> returns <code>(1, 5)</code></li><li><code>longest_rise([5, 4, 3])</code> returns <code>(0, 1)</code> (it never rises: every day is a stretch of 1, and the first one wins)</li><li><code>longest_rise([1, 2, 3])</code> returns <code>(0, 3)</code></li><li><code>longest_rise([7])</code> returns <code>(0, 1)</code></li></ul>",
   "answerpreload": "def longest_rise(temps):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(longest_rise([25, 20, 21, 24, 26, 30, 28, 29]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(longest_rise([25, 20, 21, 24, 26, 30, 28, 29]))",
