   "xml_index": 6,
   "xml_name": "PS9_006_Best Days To Buy And Sell",
   "display_name": "Best Days To Buy And Sell",
   "questiontext_html": "<p>Riya follows the price of one share of a company for several days. She wants to know on which day she <b>should have bought</b> one share, and on which <b>later</b> day she should have sold it, to make the <b>biggest profit</b> (selling price minus buying price).</p>\n<p>The days are numbered <b>from 1</b>: <code>prices[0]</code> is the price on day 1, <code>prices[1]</code> on day 2, and so on. For example:</p>\n<table style=\"border-collapse:separate;border-spacing:2px 0;margin:6px 0 4px\"><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace\">prices =</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">7</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(40,109,167);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">5</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:rgb(65,149,209);color:#fff;border-radius:2px\">6</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:5px 7px;white-space:nowrap;font-family:monospace;font-size:14px;background:#e2e6ea;color:#8a939c;border-radius:2px\">4</td></tr><tr><td style=\"padding-right:10px\"></td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">0</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">1</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">2</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">3</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">4</td><td style=\"text-align:center;padding:1px 6px;font-size:12px;color:#8a939c\">5</td></tr></table><div style=\"font-size:13px;color:#8a939c;margin:0 0 10px\">buy on day 2 at 1, sell on day 5 at 6: a profit of 5 (index 1 is day 2, index 4 is day 5)</div>\n<p><b>Your task:</b> write a function <code>best_trade(prices)</code> that <b>returns</b> a tuple <code>(buy_day, sell_day)</code> with the biggest profit, where the sell day must come <b>after</b> the buy day. Rules:</p>\n<ul><li>If several pairs of days give the same biggest profit, choose the <b>earliest buy day</b>, and for that buy day the <b>earliest sell day</b>.</li><li>If <b>no</b> profit is possible at all (the price never goes up, or there are fewer than 2 days), return <code>None</code>.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Hint:</b> the simplest way is to try every pair: every buy day <code>i</code>, and every later sell day <code>j</code>, keeping the best pair found so far. If you change the best pair only when the profit is <b>strictly</b> bigger, the earliest pair wins on ties automatically.</p>\n<p><b>Examples:</b></p>\n<ul><li><code>best_trade([7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4])</code> returns <code>(2, 5)</code></li><li><code>best_trade([3, 8, 1, 6])</code> returns <code>(1, 2)</code> (days 1 to 2 and days 3 to 4 both give 5: the earlier buy day wins)</li><li><code>best_trade([9, 7, 7, 2])</code> returns <code>None</code></li></ul>",
   "answerpreload": "def best_trade(prices):\n    # write your code here\n\n\n# ---- Use this space to test your code; comment it out before Check ----\n# print(best_trade([7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4]))\n",
   "mark": 1.0,
   "tests": [
    {
     "testcode": "print(best_trade([7, 1, 5, 3, 6, 4]))",
     "stdin": "",
     "expected": "(2, 5)",
     "extra": "",
     "show": true,
     "example": true,
     "in_precheck": true,
     "in_check": true
    },
    {
